3    The complete period set
Corollary 3. At n = 1 the sole state is fixed. For n ≥ 2 the attained periods are

                            {1, . . . , 2n} ∪ {2n + 2, 2n + 4, . . . , 4n − 4},                 (3)

where a reversed range is empty. The maximum is 4n − 4.

Proof. Write s ≤ n for core size. Odd periods beyond 1 come from short-cycle rows: the short
figure-eight gives 3, and a short barbell has p = 2s + 2 − (a + b) ≤ 2s, with odd value 2s − 1. A
nonshort figure-eight or nontrivial theta has even period 2s + 2 ≤ 4n − 4 for s ≥ 3. A nonshort
barbell has a + b ≥ 4 and p = 4s + 4 − 2(a + b) ≤ 4s − 4. The n = 2 cases follow directly from
the table.
    Periods 1, 2, 3 arise from a double loop, triple-direct theta, and figure-eight (1, 2). For
2 ≤ k ≤ n, a barbell (a, b, c) = (1, 1, k − 1) has s = k and period 2k. For 2 ≤ k ≤ n − 1,
the barbell (1, 2, k − 1) has s = k + 1 and period 2k + 1. These give all values through 2n. A
theta (1, 1, s − 1), 3 ≤ s ≤ n, realizes half-periods 4, . . . , n + 1. For each 6 ≤ k ≤ 2n − 2, put
c = max(1, k − n − 1) and r = k − 2c. Then r ≥ 4 and s = r + c − 1 ≤ n: if k ≤ n + 2, s = k − 2;
otherwise s = n and r = 2n + 2 − k ≥ 4. The barbell (1, r − 1, c) realizes period 2k. Together
these cover every remaining even value; the small values of n are included by the stated empty
ranges. Pad any smaller core by inactive fixed loops. For sharpness use the two-loop barbell at
n = 2, theta (1, 1, 2) at n = 3, and barbell (1, 3, n − 3) for n ≥ 4.


